<p>This page does not repeat the rules; the reference lists those. It says which form to choose when more than one works, and why. Some rules the compiler enforces, the rest are habit, and each section says which.</p>
<p>Every program here was run with the compiler and shows its real output. Run <code>bayan format</code> on your code: it rewrites input forms (a declaration in the wrong order, a stray <code>;</code>) into the canonical spelling, so style never needs arguing.</p>

<h2 id="names">Names</h2>
<p>Types are <code>PascalCase</code>. Everything else is <code>camelCase</code>: fields, locals, parameters, methods, enum members, union cases and error cases. Interfaces start with <code>I</code>; acronyms keep their case (<code>HttpClient</code>). A <code>PascalCase</code> function is a compile error (BYN2009).</p>
<p>The rest follows from one idea: a name says what kind of thing it is.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Thing</th><th>Name</th><th>Example</th></tr>
<tr><td>a property: a noun with no arguments</td><td>no parentheses</td><td><code>cart.total</code>, <code>list.length</code>, <code>name.runeCount</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>a call: it needs an argument</td><td>the verb</td><td><code>x.pow(2)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>yes or no</td><td>starts with <code>is</code>, <code>has</code> or <code>can</code></td><td><code>isEmpty</code>, <code>hasDiscount</code>; never <code>empty</code> for a <code>bool</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>changes the receiver</td><td>plain verb, with <code>ref this</code></td><td><code>add</code>, <code>append</code>, <code>sort</code>, <code>deposit</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>returns a new value</td><td>past participle</td><td><code>trimmed()</code>, <code>replaced(…)</code>, <code>uppercased()</code> return a changed view or copy, original untouched; <code>sort()</code> sorts in place</td></tr>
<tr><td>may give nothing</td><td>starts with <code>try</code></td><td><code>i64.tryParse(s)</code> returns <code>i64?</code>; <code>i64.parse(s)</code> fails</td></tr>
</table>
<p>A <code>ref this</code> method cannot be called on a <code>let</code>, and the signature shows the call changes the struct. Offer the <code>try</code>/plain pair when callers differ in what they want.</p>
<example name="guide-ef-names"/>
<p>Do not add <code>get</code> to a noun (<code>getTotal</code> says nothing <code>total</code> does not), and do not name a conversion <code>toList</code> or <code>asSpan</code>. The compiler rejects the second:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN5009:</span> `toList` spells a conversion with `to`; write `List(cart)`
  --&gt; naming.byn:4:22

  4 │     public List&lt;i64&gt; toList() uses alloc =&gt; prices.copy()
    │                      ^^^^^^
    │
  help: use a view noun, `copy()`, or a `T(x)` conversion

✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings
  explain: bayan explain BYN5009</pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler. The path is shortened.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>A view is a noun (<code>xs.span</code>), an owned copy is <code>copy()</code>, and a conversion is the type applied to a value (<code>i32(a)</code>). Do not abbreviate: <code>message</code>, not <code>msg</code>.</p>

<h2 id="particles">Particles: label the second argument</h2>
<p>A function with more than one required parameter labels all but the first with one of eight words: <code>from</code>, <code>to</code>, <code>until</code>, <code>through</code>, <code>at</code>, <code>by</code>, <code>for</code>, <code>as</code>. Choose the word that makes the call read as a sentence, and use each at most once per signature.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Particle</th><th>Means</th><th>Call</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>at</code></td><td>a position or key</td><td><code>m.tryGet(at: name)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>by</code></td><td>the method, separator or measure</td><td><code>s.parts(by: ",")</code>, <code>find(names, by: "fa")</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>from</code>, <code>to</code></td><td>a source and a destination</td><td><code>xs.copy(to: arena)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>until</code>, <code>through</code></td><td>an end that is excluded, or included</td><td><code>remove(from: a, through: b)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>as</code></td><td>the form something is shown or read in</td><td><code>i64.tryParse(s, as: .hex)</code></td></tr>
</table>
<p>The compiler checks that the label is there (BYN5006) and on the right argument (BYN5005); choosing the word is your job. The <a href="parameters.html">parameters page</a> has the full rules.</p>

<h2 id="choose-failure">Fail, optional or panic</h2>
<p>Three ways to say "this did not work", each with one use.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Use</th><th>When</th><th>Because</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>T?</code></td><td>Nothing there is a normal answer: a lookup, a search, an optional field.</td><td>The caller supplies a fallback with <code>??</code>. There is nothing to explain.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>fails E</code></td><td>The caller asked for something and could not get it, and the reason matters: bad input, a missing file.</td><td>The caller must handle each case or pass it up with <code>try</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>panic</td><td>The program has a bug: an index out of range, a broken precondition.</td><td>No caller can fix it. Use <code>expects</code> to state a precondition.</td></tr>
</table>
<example name="guide-ef-forms"/>
<p>A lookup that finds nothing is not an error; a port that is not a number is. Do not make a function <code>fails</code> if "not found" is its only failure and callers always default it, and do not return <code>null</code> when the caller needs the reason. Public functions list their error sets; private ones write a bare <code>fails</code> and the compiler infers.</p>
<p>Name each error case for what went wrong (<code>notNumber</code>, <code>outOfRange</code>), document it, and keep the set small enough for a caller to <code>catch switch</code> it. See <a href="errors.html">Errors</a>.</p>

<h2 id="shapes">struct, union or enum</h2>
<p>Data is a struct, and a struct is a value. Pick the type by how many shapes the value can take.</p>
<table>
<tr><td>one shape, many fields</td><td><code>struct</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>a fixed set of names with nothing attached</td><td><code>enum</code>: it has <code>name</code> and <code>ordinal</code>, and <code>Color.values</code> lists them</td></tr>
<tr><td>one of several shapes, each with its own fields</td><td><code>union</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>a set of ways to fail</td><td><code>union error</code></td></tr>
</table>
<example name="guide-ef-state"/>
<p>Prefer a union to a struct with a flag and optional fields: the union cannot say "circle" while the radius is <code>null</code>. When the shapes are closed, prefer it to an interface: a <code>switch</code> over the union lists every case, so adding one breaks each <code>switch</code> that forgot it. Use an interface when callers outside your package add their own types.</p>
<p>Reach for tools that make bad states unwritable: a <code>type distinct</code> keeps two integer ids from being mixed up, and a <code>requires</code> clause checks a value when it is built.</p>
<example name="guide-ef-units"/>
<p>Passing an <code>OrderId</code> where a <code>UserId</code> is wanted is a compile error; reach the integer with <code>.value</code>. Use these for ids, money, ports and anything where "it is only a number" caused a bug. See <a href="features.html#requires">What makes Bayan different</a>.</p>

<h2 id="bindings">let first, var when needed</h2>
<p>Start every binding as <code>let</code>; change it to <code>var</code> when the compiler says the value must change. A <code>var</code> never reassigned warns (BYN2311), and a <code>let</code> assigned twice is an error (BYN2104). Name a value once. A same-type shadow is a warning; shadowing with <code>let</code> is allowed only when the type changes: <code>let n = i64.tryParse(n) ?? 0</code> is fine.</p>
<example name="guide-ef-letvar"/>
<p>Keep a <code>var</code>'s scope small: compute a value, bind it with <code>let</code>, and use that name afterward, so the reader sees which names can change.</p>

<h2 id="guards">Leave early with a guard</h2>
<p>Put the failing cases first and end the line with the condition. A postfix <code>if</code> or <code>unless</code> fits one simple statement: <code>return</code>, <code>fail</code>, <code>break</code>, <code>continue</code>, <code>panic</code>, a call or an assignment. The rest is the main path, with no nesting.</p>
<example name="guide-ef-guard"/>
<p>Use a block <code>if</code> for two or more statements, or an <code>else</code>. Write <code>unless</code> for a plain positive condition and <code>if</code> otherwise; <code>unless not x</code> is an error. To exit on an empty optional, bind it with <code>let v = x ?? return</code> (or <code>?? fail .missing</code>) instead of nesting <code>if x is not null</code>.</p>

<h2 id="params">Read, ref or move</h2>
<p>A plain parameter is the default and almost always right: the compiler passes small values by copy and large ones by reference, and the callee cannot change them. Choose the others for a reason.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Parameter</th><th>When</th></tr>
<tr><td>plain</td><td>the function only reads; take a <code>ReadOnlySpan&lt;T&gt;</code> for a collection, so callers pass a list, an array or a slice</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>ref</code></td><td>the function changes the caller's value; write <code>ref</code> at the call too</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>move</code></td><td>the function keeps or consumes the value; after the call the caller's name is gone</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Return a new value rather than take a <code>ref</code> when the result is the point: a function that reads its input and returns an answer composes; one that edits an argument does not.</p>

<h2 id="expressions">Expression bodies and switch</h2>
<p>When a function or property is one expression, use <code>=&gt;</code>: <code>bool isEmpty =&gt; prices.length == 0</code>; when it needs a statement, use a block. A <code>switch</code> that produces a value is an expression (<code>x switch { … }</code>), one that does things is a statement. Use the expression form with a <code>return</code> or a binding, and leave out <code>default</code> when you can: then a new union case is a compile error at every switch that forgot it.</p>
<p>Use <code>if</code> for one condition and <code>switch</code> for a value with several cases; use the ternary <code>a ? b : c</code> only for a short choice between two values.</p>

<h2 id="cleanup">Clean up where you acquire</h2>
<p>Put the <code>defer</code> on the line after the thing it undoes, so the pair reads together and survives every exit: <code>return</code>, <code>fail</code> and <code>break</code>. Defers run last in, first out. <code>defer failing</code> undoes work only if the function fails, such as removing a half-written file.</p>
<p>Most memory needs no <code>defer</code>: a <code>List</code> or <code>Map</code> frees itself when its owner goes away. When many values share a lifetime, such as everything built while handling one request, use an arena (<code>using var arena = Arena()</code>) and free it with one <code>defer arena.reset()</code>. See <a href="memory.html">Memory and ownership</a>.</p>

<h2 id="effects">Say what a function touches</h2>
<p>A public function that allocates, reads files or runs programs lists it in a <code>uses</code> clause: <code>uses alloc</code>, <code>uses fs</code>, <code>uses process</code>. Private functions and <code>main</code> have it inferred. Do not hide an effect behind a global: pass the allocator with <code>provide</code>, or take a parameter that does the work, and the signature stays honest. With no <code>uses</code> clause a function is pure: easy to test, and it cannot surprise its caller.</p>

<h2 id="tests">Tests live next to the code</h2>
<p>Write a <code>test "name" { … }</code> block under the function it checks, named with a sentence that says what is true: <code>"rejects out of range"</code>. Use <code>assert</code> for the check and <code>assert f() fails E.caseName</code> for an expected error; <code>@cases(…)</code> runs one test over a table of inputs. Run them with <code>bayan test</code>. See the <a href="cookbook-testing.html">Testing cookbook</a>.</p>

<h2 id="small">Keep to the plain parts</h2>
<ul>
<li>Do not build a deep tree of interfaces; a struct with a few methods is enough for most programs.</li>
<li>Do not store references in structs to link nodes. For trees and graphs, keep the nodes in one list and refer to them by a <code>type distinct</code> index.</li>
<li>Do not use <code>unsafe</code> or <code>because "…"</code> to silence the compiler; each bypass needs a reason, and <code>bayan audit</code> lists them all.</li>
<li>Prefer the standard library's names: <code>length</code>, <code>isEmpty</code>, <code>append</code>, <code>tryGet</code>, <code>contains</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Coming from another language? The <a href="from-python.html">Python</a>, <a href="from-go.html">Go</a>, <a href="from-rust.html">Rust</a> and <a href="from-typescript.html">TypeScript</a> guides map your habits to these forms.</p>
